\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1}{\ignorespaces FCC-32 ice-band thermodynamics. (a) Periodic and transport-masked heat capacities at the sign-free coupling $J_{\pm }/J_{zz}=+0.046$, compared with the QMC data of Ref.\nobreakspace  {}\cite  {Huang2018}. Both numerical curves contain the same 2970 self-consistent FCC-32 roots. (b) Dominant heat-capacity peak across the negative-coupling scan. Every point is a paired periodic/masked calculation on the same depth-$d=1$ microscopic space; the scan stops where either complete 2970-root band ceases to be resolved. The guides show the false torus scale $g_4$ and physical hexagon scale $g_6$.}}{3}{figure.1}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:thermodynamics}{{1}{3}{FCC-32 ice-band thermodynamics. (a) Periodic and transport-masked heat capacities at the sign-free coupling $\Jpm /\Jzz =+0.046$, compared with the QMC data of Ref.~\cite {Huang2018}. Both numerical curves contain the same 2970 self-consistent FCC-32 roots. (b) Dominant heat-capacity peak across the negative-coupling scan. Every point is a paired periodic/masked calculation on the same depth-$d=1$ microscopic space; the scan stops where either complete 2970-root band ceases to be resolved. The guides show the false torus scale $g_4$ and physical hexagon scale $g_6$}{figure.1}{}}
\@writefile{toc}{\contentsline {subsection}{\numberline {B}Longitudinal response at every momentum}{3}{section*.8}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{sec:szz}{{IV\,B}{3}{}{section*.8}{}}
\newlabel{eq:bordered}{{8}{3}{}{equation.4.8}{}}
\newlabel{eq:probe}{{9}{3}{}{equation.4.9}{}}
\newlabel{eq:szz}{{10}{3}{}{equation.4.10}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {2}{\ignorespaces Longitudinal dynamical structure factor along $\Gamma $--$X$--$L$--$\Gamma $ at $J_{\pm }/J_{zz}=-0.20$. The eight FCC-32 momenta form three high-symmetry stars -- one $\Gamma $, three $X$, four $L$ -- and each block is one star, averaged over its symmetry-equivalent members; the weights within a star agree to $3\times 10^{-14}$, so the average discards nothing. Blocks are deliberately not interpolated: these are the only momenta the cluster resolves, and both nonzero stars lie on the zone boundary, so no small-$\bm  {q}$ information exists to disperse through. Each block carries a single dominant excitation ($88$--$100\%$ of the weight), consistent with the two transverse photon polarizations being degenerate at both $X$ and $L$. (a) Naive periodic dynamics and (b) the transport-masked theory use the same depth-$d=1$ space, 120 final states, four sublattice probes, Gaussian width $\eta /J_{zz}=1.2\times 10^{-3}$, and common color scale. Periodic dynamics produces strong inelastic response at $\Gamma $ and a higher apparent excitation scale. The mask removes the $\Gamma $ response while retaining finite weight at both nonzero stars. The removed weight is not in the $U(1)$-protected channel: $\DOTSB \sum@ \slimits@ _i S_i^z$ commutes with $H$, so the total longitudinal probe gives exactly zero at $\Gamma $ in both theories ($<10^{-31}$), and the periodic $\Gamma $ intensity resides entirely in the three sublattice-staggered $\bm  {q}=0$ channels, which nothing protects.}}{4}{figure.2}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:dssf}{{2}{4}{Longitudinal dynamical structure factor along $\Gamma $--$X$--$L$--$\Gamma $ at $\Jpm /\Jzz =-0.20$. The eight FCC-32 momenta form three high-symmetry stars -- one $\Gamma $, three $X$, four $L$ -- and each block is one star, averaged over its symmetry-equivalent members; the weights within a star agree to $3\times 10^{-14}$, so the average discards nothing. Blocks are deliberately not interpolated: these are the only momenta the cluster resolves, and both nonzero stars lie on the zone boundary, so no small-$\bq $ information exists to disperse through. Each block carries a single dominant excitation ($88$--$100\%$ of the weight), consistent with the two transverse photon polarizations being degenerate at both $X$ and $L$. (a) Naive periodic dynamics and (b) the transport-masked theory use the same depth-$d=1$ space, 120 final states, four sublattice probes, Gaussian width $\eta /\Jzz =1.2\times 10^{-3}$, and common color scale. Periodic dynamics produces strong inelastic response at $\Gamma $ and a higher apparent excitation scale. The mask removes the $\Gamma $ response while retaining finite weight at both nonzero stars. The removed weight is not in the $U(1)$-protected channel: $\sum _i S_i^z$ commutes with $H$, so the total longitudinal probe gives exactly zero at $\Gamma $ in both theories ($<10^{-31}$), and the periodic $\Gamma $ intensity resides entirely in the three sublattice-staggered $\bq =0$ channels, which nothing protects}{figure.2}{}}
\@writefile{toc}{\contentsline {subsection}{\numberline {C}What the longitudinal probe can and cannot see}{4}{section*.9}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{sec:parity}{{IV\,C}{4}{}{section*.9}{}}
\newlabel{eq:parity}{{11}{4}{}{equation.4.11}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3}{\ignorespaces Electric and magnetic probes reach disjoint sets of levels. (a), (b) Every computed level of the masked block, drawn at its own energy in the column of its momentum star, coloured by which probe reaches it and with bar width encoding the weight; levels reached by neither probe are shown as hairlines so that they remain visible. (c) The electric weight of each level against its magnetic weight. Points pin to one axis or the other over more than twenty-five decades and the diagonal region, where a level reached by both probes would sit, is empty. This is Eq.\nobreakspace  {}(\ref  {eq:parity}) at work rather than a numerical coincidence.}}{5}{figure.3}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:parity}{{3}{5}{Electric and magnetic probes reach disjoint sets of levels. (a), (b) Every computed level of the masked block, drawn at its own energy in the column of its momentum star, coloured by which probe reaches it and with bar width encoding the weight; levels reached by neither probe are shown as hairlines so that they remain visible. (c) The electric weight of each level against its magnetic weight. Points pin to one axis or the other over more than twenty-five decades and the diagonal region, where a level reached by both probes would sit, is empty. This is Eq.~(\ref {eq:parity}) at work rather than a numerical coincidence}{figure.3}{}}
